\ChangesListline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(rev)}{For the generation of this heatmap, balanced weight values ($w_1 = 0.4, w_2 = 0.3, w_3 = 0.3$) were used to assign equal importance to all criteria while slightly prioritising numerical calibration.}{23}
\ChangesListline {replaced}{Replaced\nobreakspace {}(rev)}{recognising}{23}
\ChangesListline {replaced}{Replaced\nobreakspace {}(rev4)}{The gTransformer model, by contrast, uses its high capacity to capture temporal learning dynamics (the individualized parameters extracted from interaction history) to differentiate students even when their observable responses are identical. By synthesising this history into a latent context vector and projecting it onto student-specific parameters ($p_{L_0}$ and $p_{T}$), the model identifies unique learning signatures that Markovian transitions fundamentally cannot represent. This allows gTransformer to differentiate students even when their immediate observable responses are identical.}{23}
\ChangesListline {replaced}{Replaced\nobreakspace {}(rev)}{categorised}{23}
\ChangesListline {replaced}{Replaced\nobreakspace {}(rev)}{grey}{24}
\ChangesListline {replaced}{Replaced\nobreakspace {}(rev)}{coloured}{24}
\ChangesListline {replaced}{Replaced\nobreakspace {}(rev)}{personalisation}{24}
\ChangesListline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(rev3bcom1)}{By providing differentiated diagnostics based on longitudinal interaction histories, the model acknowledges that students are dynamic learners with unique contexts rather than static entities defined by fixed traits, thereby enabling more personalised instructional interventions. For instance, high-momentum learners can be identified for accelerated pacing to maintain engagement, while students whose profiles indicate a need for foundational remediation can receive targeted scaffolding before significant learning gaps emerge.}{26}
\ChangesListline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(rev3bcom1)}{In general, the balance between accuracy and interpretability provided by our gTransformer model is crucial for promoting transparency and trust. This allows educators to clearly understand the pedagogical rationale behind the model's estimations, even for students with similar response patterns. Thus, our approach offers a practical path toward building AI tools that are not only accurate but also student-centred and pedagogically justifiable.}{26}
\ChangesListline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(rev4)}{The Representational Grounding methodology explored in this work is fundamentally domain-independent, which suggests that the resulting pedagogical insights and the accuracy--interpretability trade-off could be generalised to diverse educational contexts.}{26}
\ChangesListline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(rev4)}{Nonetheless, extending the empirical validation of \textit {gTransformer} beyond the benchmark datasets utilised in this study to a wider range of domains and subjects represents a valuable research direction to further demonstrate its versatility.}{26}
\ChangesListline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(rev4)}{In this initial study, the grounding process was restricted to the two primary knowledge parameters: initial mastery $P(L_0)$ and learning rate $P(T)$.}{26}
\ChangesListline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(rev4)}{Future research could address the impact of incorporating student-specific \textit {Guess} and \textit {Slip} values to further refine the granularity of context-aware individualisation.}{26}
\ChangesListline {replaced}{Replaced\nobreakspace {}(rev)}{generalisable}{26}
\ChangesListline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(rev)}{While gTransformer was validated using BKT as the primary reference model, the underlying Representational Grounding approach is inherently extensible. Within the educational domain, future research could investigate the integration of alternative theoretical frameworks, such as Additive Factor Models or Performance Factor Analysis, allowing for the use of diverse pedagogical parameters while leveraging the Transformer architecture's awareness of the historical learning context. Furthermore, the applicability of the gTransformer approach extends beyond Knowledge Tracing. The Representational Grounding framework can be adapted to any scientific field where established theoretical models coexist with high-volume empirical data. By anchoring latent representations to domain-specific concepts, this methodology offers a \replaced [id=rev]{generalisable}{generalizable} path toward bridging deep learning expressiveness with rigorous scientific grounding. Evaluating how different theoretical assumptions influence both the accuracy--interpretability trade-off and the context-awareness of domain-specific predictions across diverse disciplines remains an open and promising avenue for future research.}{26}
\ChangesListline {replaced}{Replaced\nobreakspace {}(rev)}{internalises}{26}
\ChangesListline {replaced}{Replaced\nobreakspace {}(rev)}{organising}{26}
\ChangesListline {replaced}{Replaced\nobreakspace {}(rev)}{personalisation}{26}
\ChangesListline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(rev3bcom1)}{By grounding high-capacity models in established domain theory, this approach offers a path toward highly accurate, pedagogically justifiable, and context-aware personalisation.}{27}
